    "question": "Explain BFS vs DFS with a Mermaid diagram showing the tree structure and search traversal order.",
    "answer": "**Direct answer:**  \nBreadth‑First Search (BFS) explores a tree level‑by‑level, guaranteeing the shallowest goal but using large memory, while Depth‑First Search (DFS) follows one branch to depth before backtracking, using far less memory but not guaranteeing the shallowest solution.  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Concepts  \n\n| Aspect | **Breadth‑First Search (BFS)** | **Depth‑First Search (DFS)** |\n|--------|--------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| **Exploration order** | Visits all nodes at depth 0, then depth 1, then depth 2, … | Follows a single path down to the deepest node before backtracking |\n| **Completeness** | **Complete** in finite state spaces (will find a goal if one exists)【4】 | Complete only in finite state spaces; not complete in infinite spaces【4】 |\n| **Optimality** | Optimal when path cost is a non‑decreasing function of depth; otherwise not guaranteed【2】 | Not optimal in either finite or infinite spaces【4】 |\n| **Memory usage** | High – must store every frontier node (exponential in depth)【2】 | Low – stores only the current path (linear in depth) |\n| **Typical use‑case** | When the shallowest solution is required and memory is sufficient | When memory is constrained or the tree is very deep |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Traversal Illustration  \n\nThe diagram below shows a simple binary tree and the order in which BFS and DFS visit the nodes. Numbers indicate the visitation sequence.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    %% Tree structure\n    A[\"A\"] --> B[\"B\"]\n    A --> C[\"C\"]\n    B --> D[\"D\"]\n    B --> E[\"E\"]\n    C --> F[\"F\"]\n    C --> G[\"G\"]\n\n",
